      en: 'A short trawler ride down the Pasur River from Mongla brings you to Karamjal, a tourism and wildlife-breeding centre in the Chandpai range of the eastern Sundarbans. For travellers without time for a multi-day ship tour, it is the easiest and most popular way to get a first taste of the world’s largest mangrove forest, and it works perfectly as a day trip.\n\nA winding wooden boardwalk leads through dense stands of gewa, goran and keora, with pneumatophores poking up from the mud. Spotted deer and rhesus macaques are almost guaranteed. The crocodile breeding centre shows saltwater crocodiles from hatchlings to large adults, and there is also an enclosure where deer are raised. A watchtower lets you look out over the forest canopy.\n\nAt low tide the mudflats come alive with crabs, mudskippers and wading birds. November to March brings calm water and comfortable weather, but weekends and holidays can be crowded.',
